WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.167
Linear search checks one position at a time. To find five here, inspect seven, then three, then five. Return index two, not the value five. Stop at the first match. No array value moves.

00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:32.792
Binary search depends on sorted order. In this ascending array, the midpoint is index two, holding five. We are searching for seven. Because five is too small, every value at or before that midpoint is also too small.

00:00:32.792 --> 00:00:49.250
Set low to mid plus one, which is three. High stays four. The next midpoint is three, holding seven, so return three. We discarded candidates. We did not move, swap, or delete array elements.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:01:07.417
For a missing key, continue until the candidate interval is empty. Searching for six probes five, then seven. Low becomes three and high becomes two. Bounds crossed, so return minus one. A single remaining candidate must still be checked.

00:01:07.417 --> 00:01:29.292
For one search in unsorted data, a linear scan avoids unnecessary sorting. For many searches in already sorted array data, binary search is useful. Remember: sorting can change original positions. Do not silently return a sorted index when the question asks for the original index.
